      "extracellular": true,
      "functional_impact_measured": "Tagged constructs were used in functional studies of inhibitory synapse formation (PMC3678166); surface expression of the Myc-tagged protein was confirmed by immunostaining of non-permeabilized cells, and the EGFP-tagged construct was used to visualize IgSF9b at synapses. Both tags were tolerated for surface trafficking and synaptic localization.",
      "gene_symbol": "IGSF9B",
      "insert_after_residue": 28,
      "library": null,
      "linker": null,
      "loop_run_position": null,
      "median_conservation": null,
      "numbered_against": null,
      "plddt": null,
      "position_evidence": "validated",
      "position_frame": null,
      "provenance": "literature_retrieved",
      "rationale": "L1 explicitly states the tag was inserted 'between residues 28 and 29 of IgSF9b' in the full-length, membrane-anchored protein, making this a validated exact-site insertion. Python lookup confirms residues[28]='E' and residues[29]='E' in the canonical sequence; the junction is 8 residues into the mature ectodomain (SP cleavage after residue 20), well within the extracellular span (21-726) and 699 residues from the TM helix (727-744). Both a short epitope tag (Myc, 10 aa) and a large globular domain (EGFP, ~239 aa) were tolerated, strongly supporting steric permissiveness for a ~13-23 aa ALFA-type tag. Full-length membrane-anchored IgSF9b expressed in mammalian cells (pGW1 / pEGFP-N1 vectors); a Myc epitope (EQKLISEEDL, 10 aa) and, in a separate construct, EGFP (~239 aa) were each inserted between residues 28 and 29 of the full-length protein. Residue 28 lies 8 positions C-terminal to the signal-peptide cleavage site (SP: 1-20, mature N-terminus: 21), placing the junction squarely in the extracellular N-terminal region and 699 residues from the TM boundary (727). Python verification: residues[28]='E', residues[29]='E'; both positions confirmed extracellular.",
      "residue_after": "E",
      "residue_before": "E",
      "residue_label": "E28",
